    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_9f78dd227ecf598c169873a5d41bc6ccc20501d3eb9e3b2e08b15ca528f54c72",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Goodness combines convergence in a sufficiently far-right half-plane with a scale-wise anti-degeneracy test: at every sufficiently large scale, some coefficient in the prescribed scale-dependent range must remain non-small. This converts an infinite coefficient sequence into a recurrent local test rather than a global average or a pole assertion. It gains an operational way to exclude eventual uniform coefficient smallness, but the exact range and exponent are extraction-damaged, and this span does not explain why the condition is useful in a pole proof. A parameter-dependent variant is mentioned but not developed, and the condition is identified with a hypothesis from the earlier paper.",
