    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_477d9f7c808abd60a96f2749b23b53aaae2dff2f268910ce79e9b63de9973714",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source separates two complementary inputs to its contradiction theorem. Its analytic hypotheses make a quotient controllable and, through a weighted maximum-modulus rectangle, yield an upper bound on a left vertical line. Goodness instead supplies recurrent coefficient nondegeneracy; in the later mean-square application that coefficient witness forces a non-small value on the same line. The pole conclusion needs their conjunction: analytic propagation alone supplies no lower obstruction, while goodness alone supplies no contour transport or pole conclusion.",
