    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_85bbf0882b83ea0aa0746719777c7741ba2838537e2f19eeb2bde85481636b0b",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Under the contradiction premise of a pole-free window, local denominator-zero counts permit selection of zero-separated Ingham lines, and logarithmic-derivative estimates turn that separation into quotient control on the horizontal boundaries. A decaying-kernel maximum-modulus step carries this control into a trimmed strip; a second, power-weighted kernel balances the vertical boundaries and transports an upper bound to a left line. The quoted mean-value theorem then converts goodness-supplied coefficient mass into a mean-square and hence pointwise lower bound on that same line, where comparison with the propagated upper bound rules out a sufficiently long pole-free window.",
