    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_ed04ceb5e63fb3e9bc4d42c4db078166f9d51132a5516fbd98a7a49be0a6b18a",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The base theorem is formulated for a good quotient built from shifted zeta factors, but the accompanying remarks relocate the proposed invariant from the Riemann zeta function itself to a constrained quotient architecture: derivatives and several ordinary, number-field, and ray-class L-function factors may be substituted, with a field-degree comparison replacing the original factor-count comparison. The concluding discussion broadens this into a provisional admissibility frontier that also requires denominator normalization and still lacks usable sufficient conditions for goodness; the stated forthcoming specialization shows one narrower shifted quotient where a distinguished pole implies recurrent high poles.",
